module contract;

// Fluent Bit input-chunk file-static helpers — resource-projection vocabulary
// version 1.
//
// Card NEIGHBOURS. This module declares TRUSTED ASSUMPTIONS about the two
// `static` helpers `flb_input_chunk_create` calls in its own translation unit.
// It verifies nothing: the C bodies are not analyzed by this loader, and a
// declaration is a premise. A function-level `Safe` that rests on this module
// is `Safe` RELATIVE TO these declarations and is never a proof of the C
// implementation.
//
// Both bodies were read at Fluent Bit
// `66910c10a4d7eafa810b84229cf2dcf7b0f26f97` and are cited by line.
//
// ## Both callees are `static`, and that is deliberate, not an oversight
//
// `generate_chunk_name` and `input_chunk_write_header` are file-static
// definitions inside `src/flb_input_chunk.c`, which is itself a unit of the
// pinned corpus. A declared contract ALWAYS wins over a W4-inferred summary,
// and a disagreement between the two is reported as a `SummaryConflict`
// diagnostic, never silently resolved (`crates/qcore-c-gateway/src/legacy_summary.rs`).
// Declaring a defined function does not stop the unit analyzing that function's
// own body; it only fixes what its CALLERS may assume. Card PROD-CONTRACTS
// declared `flb_input_chunk_get_name` under exactly this arrangement and
// measured zero custody summary conflicts corpus-wide; this card re-measures
// the same count with these two added.
//
// ## `generate_chunk_name`, read from `src/flb_input_chunk.c:743-754`
//
//     static void generate_chunk_name(struct flb_input_instance *in,
//                                     char *out_buf, int buf_size)
//     {
//         struct flb_time tm;
//         (void) in;
//         flb_time_get(&tm);
//         snprintf(out_buf, buf_size - 1, "%i-%lu.%4lu.flb",
//                  getpid(), tm.tm.tv_sec, tm.tm.tv_nsec);
//     }
//
// That is the whole body.
//
//   * `in` is DISCARDED by an explicit `(void) in;` on every path. No custody,
//     no read, no retention.
//   * `out_buf` is WRITTEN THROUGH by `snprintf`. Nothing is allocated for it,
//     nothing is freed through it, and the pointer is stored nowhere.
//   * `buf_size` is an `int` by value.
//   * `flb_time_get(&tm)` takes the address of a function-LOCAL
//     `struct flb_time`, not of anything the caller owns.
//   * the result is `void`.
//
// A callee that writes through a caller's buffer is not thereby taking custody
// of it, and `resource_borrowed` says only the latter. This is the same reading
// card PROD-CONTRACTS recorded for `flb_routes_mask_set_by_tag`'s
// `routes_mask`, reached the same way.
//
// NOT CLAIMED: that the write is in bounds. The body passes `buf_size - 1` as
// `snprintf`'s size while the caller passes `sizeof(name) - 1` as `buf_size`,
// and nothing here relates either number to the caller's array. Extent is not
// custody and this projection models custody only. Named
// `NEIGHBOURS-NAME-EXTENT`; it is the same class of non-claim as
// `PROD-CONTRACTS-MASK-EXTENT`, and it is neither a defect claim about the
// upstream code nor a licence to ignore one.
//
// ## `input_chunk_write_header`, read from `src/flb_input_chunk.c:1782-1852`
//
//     static int input_chunk_write_header(struct cio_chunk *chunk,
//                                         int event_type,
//                                         char *tag, int tag_len)
//
// The body allocates ONE buffer and accounts for it on BOTH exits:
//
//   * `meta = flb_calloc(1, meta_size);` (`:1807`). On the allocation-failure
//     arm it logs and returns `-1` with nothing allocated (`:1808-1811`).
//   * `memcpy(meta + FLB_INPUT_CHUNK_META_HEADER, tag, tag_len);` (`:1840`) —
//     `tag` is a memcpy SOURCE. It is read, never freed, never stored.
//   * `ret = cio_meta_write(chunk, (char *) meta, meta_size);` (`:1843`).
//     `cio_meta_write` (`lib/chunkio/src/cio_meta.c:46-73`) COPIES the buffer
//     on both backends: the memory backend `malloc`s `mf->meta_data` and
//     `memcpy`s into it (`:60-66`), and the filesystem backend forwards to
//     `cio_file_write_metadata` (`lib/chunkio/src/cio_file.c:1075-1145`), whose
//     two arms both end in `memcpy(meta, buf, size)` into the chunk's own map
//     (`:1100`, `:1141`). No callee retains `meta`.
//   * both remaining exits free it: `flb_free(meta)` on the write-failure arm
//     (`:1846`) and again on the success arm (`:1849`).
//
// So `meta` is a private local, `chunk` is written into but never freed or
// stored, `tag` is read, and the result is a `0`/`-1` status. Every parameter
// is borrowed and the result carries no custody. Over the whole v1 parameter
// vocabulary this is the only mode combination with the caller-side effect the
// body was read to have.
//
// NOT CLAIMED, recorded rather than hidden:
//
//   * **`memcpy` reads `tag_len` bytes through `tag`.** That is a validity and
//     extent requirement on the caller, not a custody fact, and this projection
//     models custody only. The declaration neither creates nor detects a caller
//     that passes a released pointer here; the gateway's own liveness
//     precondition (card BORROW-LIVENESS) is what refuses that, on settled
//     denotations, and it is unchanged by this module.
//   * **Nothing about the header's contents, the magic bytes, the truncation of
//     an over-long tag (`:1800-1803`), or whether the metadata reaches disk.**
//   * **`flb_input_chunk_write_header_v2`** (`:1854`) — the exported v2 entry
//     point beside it — is DELIBERATELY LEFT UNDECLARED. Its body is
//     substantially larger, allocates through a different route and has arms
//     this card did not read; declaring it on the strength of its neighbour's
//     shape would be exactly the unfaithful-declaration defect card P07
//     recorded. Named `NEIGHBOURS-UNDECLARED`.

// --- the chunk-file name the constructor generates into a local array ------
extern fn generate_chunk_name(in: ptr<mut, u8>, out_buf: ptr<mut, u8>, buf_size: i32) -> unit
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(in);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(out_buf);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(buf_size);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

// --- the chunk metadata header the constructor writes before publishing ----
extern fn input_chunk_write_header(chunk: ptr<mut, u8>, event_type: i32, tag: ptr<mut, u8>, tag_len: i32) -> i32
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(chunk);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(event_type);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(tag);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(tag_len);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

// ## Card CALLEE-RETENTION-CLASS1, stage (b): the raw chunk handed to the
// engine keeps its input instance
//
// USER DECISION 2026-10-05 (W48-CALLEE-DECISION-TABLE class 1, "split by
// shape"; contract spec C04.14, gateway rule 12t). Pinned (C06.2) after
// reading the body at both audited revisions (`src/flb_input_chunk.c:3559-3655`
// is identical at `66910c10` and `046accf6`; the file differs elsewhere):
//
//   * `append_to_ring_buffer(ins, …)` allocates a raw chunk, stores
//     `cr->ins = ins`, copies `tag` (`flb_sds_create_len`) and `buf`
//     (`memcpy` into `cr->buf_data`), and writes the chunk pointer into
//     `ins->rb`, retrying up to ten times; the engine thread's collector
//     dequeues it and reads `cr->ins` later. On every failure (`buf_size ==
//     0`, no memory, a full ring after the retries) the chunk is destroyed
//     (`destroy_chunk_raw`) and nothing is kept.
//   * So `ins` is retained by a holder no caller object denotes — the ring
//     buffer and then another thread — until the engine processes the chunk
//     or `flb_input_chunk_ring_buffer_cleanup` drains it at instance
//     teardown: the `global` holder. A caller can establish that only by a
//     teardown order no per-unit route decides, so every call is refused
//     (`retention-undecided`): Unknown, never Safe.
//   * `tag`, `buf`: copied, `resource_borrowed`; the scalars are data. The
//     chunk's own custody passes to the ring buffer inside the call and is
//     no caller's.
//
// NOT CLAIMED: the ring buffer's capacity, the retry timing, or that the
// engine dequeues every chunk.

// --- a raw chunk enqueued for the engine keeps `ins` ------------------------
extern fn append_to_ring_buffer(ins: ptr<mut, u8>, event_type: i32, flags: i32, records: usize, tag: ptr<const, u8>, tag_len: usize, buf: ptr<const, u8>, buf_size: usize) -> i32
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(ins);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(event_type);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(flags);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(records);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(tag);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(tag_len);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(buf);
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(buf_size);
    ensures normal resource_retained_by(ins, global);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};
